    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 061744\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n944 AM PST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n*  Breezy northerly winds are expected today and Saturday, with\n   the strongest winds with elevated wave heights in the Colorado\n   River Valley. Otherwise, quiet weather with near normal\n   temperatures are expected through the week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Thursday.\n\nA sharp upper level trough will continue to shift east today. This \nwill put the region in an area of increasing northerly flow, which \nwill result in dry conditions and breezy north winds today and \nSaturday. The pressure gradient wont be overly impressive and \nwidespread gusts 20-30 MPH gusts with stronger gusts due to terrain \nenhancement in the Colorado River Valley are expected. While \nLaughlin/Bullhead City may briefly see 40 MPH winds, northerly gusts \nin the Colorado River should remain around 30-35 MPH . Impacts to \nboating due to winds and building waves are expected on Lake Mohave \nand Lake Havasu and a Lake Wind Advisory is in effect through \nSaturday afternoon. By Monday, the trough will be lifting out of the \narea and winds will diminish with dry conditions expected through \nthe week. The coolest day should be today as high temperatures run a \ncouple degrees below normal, but as ridging builds in behind the \nexiting system, temperatures will warm through next week to near \nor slightly above normal. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Elevated \nnortherly winds persist into the early evening, with peak gusts \nbetween 20 and 25 knots. Sustained wind speeds decrease to less than \n10 knots and direction shifts to the northwest overnight. Lighter \nwinds can be expected tomorrow. VFR conditions continue through the \nTAF period. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Gusty northerly winds \ncontinue across much of the forecast area through the rest of today. \nIn the Colorado River Valley, peak gusts will be in the 25 to 35 \nknot range, decrease slightly overnight, then strengthen again \ntomorrow. In the Las Vegas Valley, peak gusts will be between 20 and \n25 knots, with lighter winds expected overnight and tomorrow. VFR \nconditions will persist through the TAF period.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...\nAVIATION...Meltzer\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
